FlowGPU v0.1 grammar

This file describes what the parser accepts today. Anything the design
intends but v0.1 does not accept is listed at the end under RESERVED, so
that the grammar makes no promise the implementation does not keep.


GRAMMAR

program: pipeline+

pipeline: "pipeline" NAME "{"
           statement*
         "}"

statement: input_decl
         | assignment
         | output_decl

input_decl: "input" NAME ":" type

output_decl: "output" NAME

// v0.2: one statement may bind several names when the operator produces
// several values. The target count is the number of names on the left; the
// multi-result operators are split, topk_last, cumsum_totals, segment_rank,
// store_cache_4d, linear_decode_forward, gated_delta_recurrence,
// argmax_last and argmin_last.
assignment: NAME ("," NAME)* "=" expression

?expression: addition

?addition: multiplication
         | addition add_op multiplication

add_op: "+" | "-"

?multiplication: atom
               | multiplication "*" atom

?atom: NAME
     | call
     | shape_literal
     | integer_literal
     | dtype_literal

// A shape literal names reshape's target shape; a dtype literal names
// cast_to's target dtype. Both are distinct expression kinds, not tensors:
// anywhere except that one operator position the type checker rejects them
// by name.
shape_literal: "[" shape "]"
integer_literal: ["-"] NUMBER
dtype_literal: dtype

call: NAME "(" [argument_list] ")"

argument_list: expression ("," expression)*

// A declaration with a bracketed shape is a tensor; one with only a dtype is
// a rank-0 scalar (one host number, never a pointer with strides).
type: tensor_type
    | scalar_type

tensor_type: dtype "[" shape "]"

scalar_type: dtype

shape: dimension ("," dimension)*

?dimension: NAME | NUMBER

?dtype: "fp16"
      | "bf16"
      | "fp32"
      | "fp8e4m3"
      | "fp8e5m2"
      | "int8"
      | "uint8"
      | "int32"
      | "int64"
      | "bool"

NAME: /[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*/
NUMBER: /[0-9]+/
COMMENT: /#[^\n]*/

%ignore WS
%ignore COMMENT


NOTES

* Statement order is significant. An `input` must precede the statements
  that read it, and a value must be defined before it is used
  (def-before-use): a forward reference is an error, not a forward
  declaration. See design 5.2.

* `output` may appear more than once. The grammar does not require it to be
  last, and design 12.3 emits two outputs.

* A name is bound exactly once. Re-binding (`h = silu(h)`) is a duplicate
  definition, because a single name must correspond to a single producer.
  Write `h2 = silu(h)`. See design 5.2.

* `pipeline`, `input`, `output` and the ten dtype names are reserved in the
  token stream. The parser lexes contextually, which still lets a reserved
  word through where the grammar expects a NAME; enforcing the reserved set
  is a name-resolution check rather than a lexical one, and is not
  implemented yet.

* Infix arithmetic is sugar for an operator call: `x - y` means
  `sub(x, y)`, and `x + y` / `x * y` mean `add(x, y)` / `mul(x, y)`. Both
  spellings reach the AST as the same node, so one computation has one
  representation in the language core (design 7.1) and its type rule lives
  in exactly one place -- the operator registry (design 10).

  Precedence and associativity come from the shape of that ladder and from
  nothing else: each level's base case is the next level up, so `*` binds
  tighter than `+` and `-`, and left recursion makes both levels
  left-associative. There is no `div`, because v0.1 has no `div` type rule:
  the operator comes first, the token follows.

* Literal dimensions must be positive (a semantic check); symbolic dimensions
  are validated before execution. RoPE requires an even last dimension.

* `rope(x, cos, sin)` has three full-shape, same-dtype floating inputs;
  it rotates paired half-axis elements. Unary `rope(x)` is not supported.

* Dimensions are matched syntactically. `bf16[2,4]` is compatible with
  `bf16[2,4]` and incompatible with `bf16[4,2]`. There is no dimension
  arithmetic, so `bf16[1+1,4]` is outside the language.

* The lexer and the parser are built from the same grammar file
  (compiler/grammar.lark), so the token stage cannot drift from the parser's
  terminals.


v0.2 (2026-10-02): multi_assignment moved into the grammar above --
`assignment: NAME ("," NAME)* "=" expression` -- because v0.1.2 implements
split, the operator it was written for. The part count is the number of
names; no v0.1 operator produces more than one result, and that is why this
was reserved then. A shape literal atom (`[N,32,64]`) also joined the
grammar for reshape's second argument.

RESERVED -- designed, not accepted

The following appeared in earlier revisions of this file as if they were
part of the language. They are not, and saying so here is the point: a
grammar that promises what the compiler rejects is worse than a smaller
grammar.

buffer_type: "Buffer" "<" dtype ">"
    Dropped on purpose rather than implemented: design 7.3 and 11 define
    Buffer as an IR-level concept that the user never writes. Having it in
    the DSL type syntax contradicted the design document itself.

`return`
    Was declared as a keyword token in both the lexer and the parser with no
    production in either and no appearance in this file. Removed.

* `attention(q,k,v)` is a three-tensor call, in two ranks. 2D keeps the
  v0.1 contract: q:[L,D], k:[S,D], v:[S,V] -> [L,V]. 4D is the
  batched-heads form: q:[B,H,L,D], k:[B,G,S,D], v:[B,G,S,V] -> [B,H,L,V],
  where G < H is GQA (H//G query heads share one key/value head; literal
  head counts are checked to divide at compile time, symbolic ones at run
  time, because H//G is arithmetic this grammar has no expression for).
  It explicitly means non-causal, dropout-free softmax(QK^T/sqrt(D))V with
  FP32 GPU intermediates and one final output dtype boundary. GPU execution
  currently requires the hybrid backend (FP32 library math by default); the CPU target implements
  its mathematical definition. It does not add scalar literals or implicit casts.
* `causal_attention(q,k,v)` takes the same two ranks with a causal mask:
  query i attends to keys 0..i+(S-L), the kv-cache offset convention
  (S >= L, checked at run time). Causal is an operator NAME, not a call
  argument, because the grammar has no boolean literal to write in an
  argument position -- rms_norm/rmsnorm_hf is the precedent for two
  conventions carried by two names.
